#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120736} :activity/id #uuid "9bad4bff-0bb7-5ce9-89c2-cbdc3bbadd77" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121058 :tutorial/content "The dimension of the column space of a matrix $A$ is called the **rank of $A$**. It's usually denoted by $\\textrm{rank}(A)$. So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Col}(A)).\n$$\n\nFor example, to find the rank of \n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n\nwe write $A$ in row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that there are $2$ pivot columns, namely, the $1$st and the $2$nd. This implies that the first and the second columns of $A$ form a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and, therefore, the dimension of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is equal to $2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6318f9cd-930b-5b7b-acc7-c27b5b241825" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3519 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "df182889-cfbe-5906-abbc-45fc241177c2" :step/math-academy-id 19977 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119661 :question/id #uuid "7cce27e9-e2b2-599c-9f4e-437caf1a23ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-5415" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4 \\times 7$ matrix that has $3$ **non-pivot** columns. Find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$. \n\nSince $A$ has $7$ columns and $3$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n7-3=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be3d5a74-259e-58b9-948d-47ba7412ab08" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673055} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673054}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Rank of a Matrix Given the Number of Pivot Columns"} :step/id #uuid "fd1734a2-5193-5182-9a6f-4a024ee32ce5" :step/math-academy-id 19978 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120761}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119662 :question/id #uuid "a6a3db41-6033-537a-bc9d-03fde02dc869" :question/math-academy-id "e-5417" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4?$\n\n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a matrix of rank $4$ must have at least $4$ columns and at least $4$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $B$ since it has only $3$ rows. \n\nAlso, the matrix must have $4$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $A$ has only $3$ pivot columns, while the matrix $C$ has exactly $4$ pivot columns. So, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3$ and $\\textrm{rank}(C)=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d847ec9-c379-5013-ab77-a9b92dc8ca27" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538150} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495078}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix With a Given Rank by Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "8cc42d44-bd5b-5eb5-9e55-ef7a7d93c31a" :step/math-academy-id 19980 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119663 :question/id #uuid "b3e4eb01-0669-586b-a716-aef87d1c3580" :question/math-academy-id "e-5418" :question/problem "If \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n, find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the rank of $A,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 35\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $3$ pivot columns (or, alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5a44c9-16e5-55fb-a10f-15435a0a9a4c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455491}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank of a Matrix Using Row Reduction"} :step/id #uuid "c27d4e1c-92bf-502f-9c02-c0bf68357dbf" :step/math-academy-id 19981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121068 :tutorial/content "Note that the number of pivot columns always coincides with the number of non-zero rows in the corresponding row echelon form matrix.\nSo, technically, \n\n$\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of *pivot columns* in the corresponding row echelon form matrix of $A$.\n\nOr, equivalently,\n\n$\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of *non-zero rows* in the corresponding row echelon form matrix of $A$." :tutorial/id #uuid "c062d466-946c-526b-aba8-0a0895671218" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4151 :tutorial/title "An Alternative Definition of the Rank"} :step/id #uuid "1383e230-aa34-5f36-a91f-c60846be8cc4" :step/math-academy-id 25259 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120738}}] :activity/title "The Rank of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120057}]}
